cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it using the metadata below."
type: software
title: "pcf-continuant: when does the dominant coefficient of a polynomial continued fraction vanish? (positivity criterion with a machine-checked core; explicit vanishing witness)"
abstract: >-
  For a real polynomial b of degree d with leading coefficient beta_d > 0, let q_n
  be the continuant denominators q_0 = 1, q_1 = b(1), q_n = b(n) q_{n-1} + q_{n-2}.
  With P_n = prod_{k<=n} b(k) and Q_n = q_n / P_n, the scaled limit
  C = R_inf = lim Q_n is the dominant connection coefficient of the seed (1, b(1)),
  and C != 0 iff q is not a minimal solution. A ratio criterion and a
  positivity-persistence lemma give: once b > 0 on a tail with two consecutive
  positive q, q is dominant and C != 0; and if b(n) > 0 for all n >= 1 then
  C >= 1 (the monotone, bounded-below engine is machine-checked in Lean 4 with a
  clean axiom cone, no sorryAx). This recovers the strict positivity
  delta = log R_inf > 0 of the companion quadratic growth-law deposit for the
  family A n^2 + B n + C_0 with integers A, B, C_0 >= 1. The universal statement
  "C != 0 for all beta_d > 0" is FALSE: an explicit quadratic
  b(n) = n^2 - 6.2803109563277...n + 8.4205764270426... has C = 0 to more than
  40 digits at 50-digit precision (sign-varying transient; b(1) > 0 does not imply
  C != 0). Only >= 1 zero is claimed: (alpha,beta) -> R_inf has genuine poles where
  b has an integer root, so sign changes are not zeros. Grades: PROVEN
  (Lean core), STRUCTURAL (elementary criteria), VERIFIED (witness, mpmath 50 digits),
  FALSE (universal claim). Whether C = 0 implies the generating function is entire is
  left open (separate unique-nearest-singularity analysis).
